**Costochondritis** (you say it "kos-toe-kon-dry-tis") is inflammation of the **cartilage** that joins your **ribs to your breastbone** (sternum). It causes pain in the front of the chest.

The pain can feel frightening because it is in the chest, but costochondritis is **not dangerous** and does not affect your heart. It usually gets better on its own.

{% callout type="danger" title="Call 999 if you have chest pain and" %}
- it feels tight, heavy or crushing, or spreads to your arms, back, neck or jaw
- you are short of breath, sweating, feeling sick or dizzy
- it started suddenly and has not gone away

These can be signs of a **heart attack** or another emergency. Do not assume it is costochondritis. If you are not sure, call **999** or **NHS 111**.
{% /callout %}

## Symptoms

- **sharp, aching or pressure-like pain** at the front of the chest, often on the **left side**
- pain that gets **worse when you move your upper body**, take a deep breath, cough, sneeze or lie down
- **tenderness** when you press on the joints where your ribs meet your breastbone
- pain in more than one rib, often the 2nd to 5th ribs

The pain can range from mild to severe. Some people find everyday tasks, such as lifting, reaching or sleeping on one side, hard while it lasts.

## What causes it?

Often there is **no clear cause**. It can follow:

- a lot of **coughing**, for example after a chest infection
- **strain** from heavy lifting, exercise or repeated movements
- an **injury** to the chest
- an **infection**

It is more common in women and in people aged over 40, but it can affect anyone. Costochondritis is also more common in people with **fibromyalgia**.

## Getting diagnosed

There is no specific test. A GP will:

- ask about your symptoms
- **press gently on your chest** to see if the rib joints are tender
- make sure the pain is **not coming from your heart or lungs**, which may mean tests such as an **ECG**, blood tests or a chest X-ray

Read about [costochondritis treatment](/conditions/musculoskeletal/costochondritis/treatment/).

{% callout type="warn" title="See a GP if" %}
- your chest pain is not getting better after a few weeks
- you have a **high temperature**, or redness and swelling over the painful area
- you have chest pain and a history of cancer, or you feel generally unwell or are losing weight without trying
{% /callout %}
